BUG-007 — Simultaneous bookings can both succeed for the same slot
Field-------Value
Severity ->	Critical
Priority -> P1
Area ->	Booking request
Found -> Concurrency
Spec reference -> SPEC.md § Requesting a booking / concurrency
Status ->	Open

Summary
Two members requesting the same space and time slot at the same moment can both successfully create bookings. This violates the critical concurrency requirement that exactly one request must succeed when two members attempt to book the same slot simultaneously.

The specification explicitly states that two members requesting the same slot at the same moment must not both succeed and that exactly one request gets the slot.

Environment
Build: Sabhaghar Booking 2.3.1
Running via: npm start, port 4000
Data state: Current challenge data
Browser: Not applicable — issue reproduced through API automation
Account used: Two authenticated member accounts
API testing: Performed using Playwright
Automation: Playwright 1.63.0
Steps to reproduce
Authenticate as Member A using POST /api/auth/login.
Authenticate as Member B using POST /api/auth/login.
Select the same available space, event date, start time, and end time for both members.
Send both POST /api/bookings requests simultaneously using Playwright Promise.all().
Capture the HTTP response status from both requests.
Observe that both requests return 201 Created.
Expected result

According to SPEC.md, when two members request the same space and time slot at the same moment:

Exactly one request should succeed with 201 Created.
The other request should be rejected with 409 Conflict.
Two bookings must not be created for the same slot.

The specification identifies this as the single most important rule for booking concurrency.

Actual result

Both simultaneous booking requests successfully created bookings.

The Playwright test produced:

Member A status: 201
Member B status: 201

Expected: 1
Received: 2

Therefore, both members were able to book the same space and time slot simultaneously.

Impact

The system can create duplicate bookings for the same space and time.

This can result in:

Double booking of the same resource.
An incorrect booking schedule.
Conflicting commitments for members.
Operational problems for staff managing the venue.

Because the specification identifies concurrency protection as the single most important booking rule, this is classified as Critical / P1.

